
[38;5;231m⏺[39m [1mClassification: architectural.[0m This repo has no React, no build tooling, and no tests — it's a minimal CommonJS script
  project ([38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m, [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m, a [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m with no dependencies). A React todo list introduces a new stack
  and new structure rather than changing a flow that already exists, so I'll go through the full process: questions →
  approaches → design → written spec → implementation plan. Say the word if you'd rather I treat it as a quick throwaway
  spike.

  Working in this repo, I'd add the React app alongside the existing files rather than replacing them ([38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m and
  [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m stay untouched).

  First question — the one that shapes the most downstream work: [1mwhere the todo data lives.[0m

  - [1mIn-memory only[0m — state resets on every refresh. Zero infrastructure, fastest to build, fine for a demo or a UI
    sandbox. Cheap to upgrade later.
  - [1mBrowser localStorage[0m — todos survive refresh, still no server. Adds a small persistence layer and a load/save
    boundary worth testing. Also cheap to upgrade later, and it's the common default for this kind of app.
  - [1mReal backend (API + database)[0m — multi-device, multi-user possible. This roughly triples the scope: server, data
    model, API contract, deployment. Not cheap to walk back, and not cheap to add later either if the client is written
    assuming synchronous local state.

  My recommendation: [1mlocalStorage[0m. It's the smallest thing that makes the app feel real, it keeps the whole project
  client-side, and it gives us a genuine seam to test. Going to a backend later means rewriting the storage module, not
  the UI — as long as we put the storage behind an interface from the start, which I'd do either way.
